Vague Pelvic Discomfort (Pending Workup)
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Dull, non-radiating lower abdominal/pelvic ache | Strictly normal bimanual pelvic examination | Absence of acute genitourinary symptoms (e.g., dysuria/bleeding)


VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-048
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal escalation of vague abdominal pain and extreme health anxiety requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an acute surgical abdomen or ectopic pregnancy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset pelvic pain in a female of reproductive age. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out acute appendicitis, ruptured ovarian cyst, or ovarian torsion, preventing potentially fatal hemorrhage or irreversible ischemic damage prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: There is this deep, constant ache sitting low in my stomach, and I am terrified it is my appendix bursting or an ectopic pregnancy.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a dull, poorly localized ache in the lower abdomen and pelvis that flared severely this evening. The pain has an intermittent severity that is not strictly tied to the menstrual cycle or bowel movements. The patient describes a lack of gross hematuria, significant dysuria, or acute severe vaginal bleeding. The patient explicitly denies severe, sharp, doubling-over pain. A home pregnancy test performed earlier today was negative.
Functional Status: Fully independent; pacing the room due to anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: High somatic anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/78 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious but is not diaphoretic or in toxic distress. An incidental review of previous presentations notes subjective muscle weakness with variable effort on testing and a normal creatine kinase (undifferentiated motor weakness/functional overlay), highlighting a pattern of somatic amplification unrelated to the acute abdominal presentation.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals normal, active bowel sounds. There is mild discomfort on deep suprapubic palpation without a palpable mass. Crucially, there is an absolute absence of rebound tenderness, severe guarding, or rigidity (negative Rovsing's, negative McBurney's). There is no severe, sharp unilateral adnexal tenderness to suggest acute torsion. External genitalia examination (declined by patient due to lack of bleeding) is deferred as the abdominal exam lacks acute peritoneal signs.
Environmental Observations: Safe, clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking over-the-counter NSAIDs with partial relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Vague Pelvic Discomfort (Pending Workup) - Status: Acute functional/somatic exacerbation without acute surgical pathology.
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out definitively due to the complete lack of focal right lower quadrant peritonitis, absence of fever, and lack of systemic toxicity.

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out clinically due to the confirmed negative urine beta-hCG, lack of hemodynamic instability, and absence of acute, severe, sharp unilateral pain.

Ovarian Torsion - Ruled out due to the dull, aching nature of the pain rather than sudden, explosive, sharp pain accompanied by intractable vomiting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden spike in pelvic pain coupled with severe patient anxiety mandated an immediate after-hours home assessment to exclude a life-threatening surgical emergency. Telephone triage appropriately identified the high risk for appendicitis or a gynecological crisis. A rigorous abdominal examination confirmed entirely soft, non-peritonitic viscera, completely ruling out an acute surgical abdomen or massive internal hemorrhage. By establishing the benign, non-acute nature of this deep pelvic ache, the clinician immediately arrested the patient's panic cycle, safely averting a highly distressing and unnecessary late-night emergency department evaluation while ensuring a coordinated outpatient imaging pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral antispasmodics and analgesia. The expected investigation results include a non-diagnostic transvaginal or transabdominal pelvic ultrasound with a negative urinalysis, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via an outpatient gynecological workup.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the dull pelvic cramping and reduce anxiety, apply a heating pad to the lower abdomen for 20 minutes while practicing slow, diaphragmatic breathing; actively relaxing the pelvic floor muscles will directly alleviate the tension contributing to the deep ache.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days for a formal pelvic ultrasound referral and routine STI screening if indicated.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops sudden, blindingly severe sharp pain in the abdomen, begins vomiting uncontrollably, or develops a high fever with chills.

